From ae0faa9ca8229379bf21542dc934fcc119f53b70 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roger Koranteng <info.uds918@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 20:14:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] A white interface, a shorter report, and the room files
 saying what they assume

The interface is white and only white. The dark theme and its toggle are gone,
and the palette is computed rather than guessed: every foreground is at least
4.5:1 against the surface it sits on, with the ratios written into the
stylesheet beside the tokens. The drawn figure now stands on ruled paper with a
metre grid, which is what the geometry is.

Taken out of the running app, because none of it was a reading about the room:
the rail's six telemetry rows, the navigation count chips, the SHA-256 artefact
table, the privacy claim repeated in the topbar, the second empty-state panel,
the escalation rungs that rendered as empty cards to say they had not fired, the
hazard "skipped" list when nothing was skipped, and a remedy line that repeated
the refusal sentence directly above it. The keypoint ledger keeps the three
figures that matter: how many points cleared the threshold, how many camera
bytes were written, and how many bytes of keypoints the run kept.

The bundled samples and the hand-set CDC rooms now carry the calibration block
their rooms always implied, so the interface reads "camera exact" or "camera
assumed, not measured" from the data instead of falling back to "unstated". The
documents said those files declared it; now they do.

report.md 669 to 412 lines and evaluation.md 485 to 380, by compression rather
than by dropping evidence: every measurement, model name, licence, OpenCV call
and named failure is still there. docs/costs.md is deleted and the hosting rates
with it; what matters about the deployment is six lines in the report.

Removed throughout: the claim that bathing, toileting and dressing are where a
large share of falls happen. It is an assertion with no citation behind it. What
the cited work supports is that camera acceptance is lowest for intimate
activities (Maidhof et al., Front Public Health 2023), and that is what the
documents now say.

The figures in the top-level README were two runs out of date: 25 of 25 exits
called, 1.23 hours of quiet, 124 tests.

